WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.579
A series adds the terms of a sequence

00:00:03.579 --> 00:00:08.347
Because we cannot finish infinitely many additions, study the partial sums instead

00:00:08.347 --> 00:00:15.500
For successive halves, the totals are one half, three quarters, seven eighths, and values increasingly close to one.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:25.665
The geometric sum formula makes the missing amount explicit: after N terms, the remaining gap is one over two to the N

00:00:25.665 --> 00:00:30.500
That gap tends to zero, so the infinite series converges to one.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.957
Terms approaching zero are necessary, but not sufficient

00:00:34.957 --> 00:00:39.297
Group the harmonic series into blocks ending at powers of two

00:00:39.297 --> 00:00:46.400
Each new block contributes at least one half, so the partial sums keep growing beyond every finite bound.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.024
Signs can change the conclusion

00:00:50.024 --> 00:00:56.997
For alternating one over n, decreasing term magnitudes tend to zero, so the series converges

00:00:56.997 --> 00:01:02.111
After N terms, its error is at most the next magnitude

00:01:02.111 --> 00:01:04.900
This bound requires those alternating-series conditions.
